>
  <p>You can have motivation, a plan and a clear desk, and still lose an evening to a phone that buzzed once. Avoiding distraction is less about trying harder in the moment and more about removing the moment entirely.</p>

  <h2 id="phone">Deal with the phone first</h2>
  <p>Studies by Adrian Ward and colleagues show your phone drains attention just by being present, even face down. Silent mode is not enough, because you still glance at it. The reliable move is distance: put it in another room, in a drawer, or hand it to a family member until your block ends. If you need it for a timer, use a separate device or the <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro timer</a> on your laptop instead.</p>

  <h2 id="environment">Design your space</h2>
  <p>Gloria Mark's research found that after an interruption it takes serious time to get back to deep work. So remove interruptions before they happen:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A fixed study spot your brain learns to associate with work, not your bed.</li>
    <li>Only the materials for the current task on the desk.</li>
    <li>A closed door and a "back at [time]" message to the people around you.</li>
    <li>Notifications off on every device, not just the phone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="internal">Internal distractions</h2>
  <p>Not every distraction is external. Worries and random ideas pull just as hard. Keep a notebook beside you and park each thought in one line, then return to the task. You are not ignoring it, you are scheduling it. If exam worry is the thing pulling at you, an <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> and a written plan often calm it more than reassurance does, because they replace a vague fear with a concrete next step.</p>

  <h2 id="online">Studying on a laptop</h2>
  <p>When the work itself is on a screen, the distraction and the task share a window. Fight back by downloading readings in advance and turning off wifi, using one browser window with one tab, and using a site blocker during blocks. Break online research into a dated plan with the <a href="/tools/assignment-planner/">assignment planner</a> so you know exactly what you are looking for before you open a browser. For the deeper focus habit, read <a href="/guides/how-to-focus-on-studies/">how to focus on studies</a>.</p>
</Guide>
